NOTES: >>>
DEPTH implemented, not verified: the assessment is complete, and every claim marked "measured" in it was run on this tree or the installed package (the end-to-end Nix reader prototype, the .nix and .ts generic-reader probes, the ELEMENT: module and option probes, the format round-trip of HASH, the grammar ABI probe, the scribe relate refusal), but no other session has re-run any of it. Every EVIDENCE node in the corpus carrying the EV- prefix at the time of filing (17 of 17) sits at implemented for the same reason; verified is a reviewer's act, not the author's.

Filed 2026-09-02 against strictdoc 0.28.3 (the dev shell's binary, /nix/store/9khac7sfda0rzk6blqd3cnlwb7q3hsvz-strictdoc) and this worktree at 2f90b224 on feat/strictdoc-trial; main at 4705317b has none of dev/scripts/sdoc_*, docs/sdoc or strictdoc_config.py. Four independent readers covered the package's registry and readers, its marker grammar and resolver, the repo's fingerprint and check tooling, and grammar packaging; their reports were reconciled into the STATEMENT, and every disagreement among them or with upstream prose is kept below rather than resolved.

Contradictions, all kept:

1. Direction of the dirty edge. The operator's phrasing ("dirties your edge to the hard coded grammar render in code") has the code-side node depending on the narrative; reader 3 holds that this reverse edge is ruled out by "nothing in the grid may depend on one" (docs/spec/nar-semantic-layer-type-narrative.sdoc:18), by MECHANISM having no Cites role (docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra:75-94) and by is_ready refusing a sketch-DEPTH parent (dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py:113-115), and that the compliant shape is NARRATIVE to File with a two-sided signature on the edge. Both positions kept; the operator owns the call.

2. Where the code-side hash lives and who reads it. strictdoc's HASH slot on the relation (a checker over sdoc_model.Graph, since strictdoc/backend/json/json_generator.py:310-323 drops HASH, or a patched exporter) versus PARENT_FP with a path key (a File branch in fp-check plus a stretch of docs/plans/strictdoc-tooling/dec-fingerprint-in-node.sdoc:12-19, "fingerprints point strictly downward", whose RETIRES_ON names exactly "per-edge state a node field cannot carry"). Readers 1 and 2 confirm the export blindness but do not choose. Undecided.

3. Edge target for the grid: grammar-groups.json (the copied prose, reader 3's recommendation and the "content never in renderer" rule) versus grammars.js or index.html (where the existing MECH-SDOC-BOARD-GRAMMARS-VIEW File edges point, docs/sdoc/board/.sdoc/mech-sdoc-board-grammars-view.sdoc:52-55).

4. Cost of enabling the native feature. The repo node measured 12.32 s vs 1.52 s per export on 890 files and says narrowing include_source_paths produces false positives (docs/plans/strictdoc-tooling/mech-file-relation-existence.sdoc:47-58, 83-109); reader 1 lists the real cost of the scan on this repo as unmeasured under its lens, and the scratch prototype ran in 1.09 s on a two-file tree with include_source_paths narrowed to its src/ directory (scratch e2e/run1.log). The 12 s figure is the only whole-tree measurement; narrowing is measured only as a false-positive risk, not as a timing.

5. Which reader is the template. Reader 1: reader_python.py (391 lines) is the smallest; reader 2: reader_c.py (539) is the most complete for a node-walking reader because of comment adjacency, decl/def and custom_tags; reader 4: reader_rust.py (729, TS_QUERY-driven) is the "load the grammar and patterns" shape the operator described. A choice of starting point, not a factual conflict; the measured prototype is closest to the Python shape.

6. Upstream docs versus upstream code on classes. The user guide (l.3205-3206) says classes are traceable in Python, C++ and others; l.3237-3238, 3260 and 3443 say scope=class and CLASS: are Python only; release 0.4.0 said C++ class markers were not yet implemented, with no later reversal. Code: Reader_C.supported_elements() returns function and class (strictdoc/backend/sdoc_source_code/reader_c.py:82-84) and the resolver matches classes by display_name for any reader (core/file_traceability_index.py:975-980). Whether the C++ reader actually emits class-scoped items was not measured.

7. The user guide's "and others" language list (l.3198-3210; L2 SDOC-SRS-142 names Ada and Gherkin) versus the code's closed suffix chain of exactly .py, the C family, .robot and .rs (reader_registry.py:34-53). Ada (#2345) and JS/TS (#2214) are open requests; no reader exists.

8. The source-nodes heading "experimental, for C/C++ and Rust only" (user guide l.3531) versus release 0.22.0 adding Robot source nodes and the registry passing custom_tags to the Robot reader (reader_registry.py:46-48). Docs stale.

9. Inside the repo: packages/strictdoc-grammar/values/spec.nix:1-2, 32-34 says "NO FILE RELATIONS ANYWHERE, deliberately ... Declaring one would owe a staleness story on day one", but that file is a proposal, not wired; the live grammar (docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra:91, 142, 196, 344, 398) declares File on five types, and the staleness story is what is now being asked for.

10. Reader 4's unknown, whether the generic reader requires scope=file in the literal top comment, is answered by code: the textX grammar matches an @relation marker on any line (strictdoc/backend/sdoc_source_code/grammar.py:24-31) and reader 1 measured a scope=file marker in a .nix file resolving (scratch exp3b). The user guide's "must be added to the top comment" (l.3243) is stricter than the generic reader enforces; language-aware readers do enforce position by their own adjacency rules.

11. Staleness of MECH-NODE-CODE-LINKAGE-IS-PROSE: docs/plans/strictdoc-tooling/mech-node-code-linkage-is-prose.sdoc:13 says 3 File relations on 2 nodes in a 144-node graph; the tree at 2f90b224 has 49 on 35 nodes across 432 nodes (measured: git ls-files '*.sdoc' piped through grep for the File TYPE line). Both figures kept; the node is dated 2026-08-27 and the growth landed after it.

12. Role-validation asymmetry (reader 1 only, from code): ForwardLanguageItemMarker inherits LanguageItemMarker and is skipped by the File-role check, while backward LineMarker and forward range and file markers are validated against the grammar's File ROLE declarations (strictdoc/core/file_traceability_index.py:1105-1127). Relevant if a role such as Implementation is put on a function-scoped marker; not contested by the other readers, not measured.

Unmeasured or unknown to the readers: the timing of a narrowed include_source_paths on this tree (item 4); whether the C++ reader emits class-scoped items (item 6); whether a backward @relation(NAR-CANON-TYPES-GRID, scope=file) marker inside a JSON $comment string parses under the generic reader, whose regex matches mid-line (grammar.py:24-31), which matters only if the native feature is on; which file the grid's edge should target (item 3); and the role-validation asymmetry's effect in practice (item 12).
